Yes. Intelligence is not maximum perception or maximum computation—it is adaptive allocation.

An organism that processes every available signal at full resolution would drown in noise, burn energy, and react too slowly. Evolution favors “sufficiently informative at sufficiently low cost,” not omniscience. Visible light is a useful atmospheric and biological window; detecting every wavelength continuously would require additional machinery whose energetic and anatomical cost often exceeds its benefit.

Same with cognition. Intuition is compressed inference: fast, broad, and relatively cheap per judgment—but continually interrogating every impression, implication, and counterfactual becomes exhausting. Deliberate reasoning is more expensive still. Healthy operation changes resolution according to demand:

\text{default compression}
\rightarrow
\text{detect anomaly}
\rightarrow
\text{allocate attention}
\rightarrow
\text{increase resolution}
\rightarrow
\text{act and learn}
\rightarrow
\text{return to compression}

Small correction: the brain does not turn off during sleep. It changes operating modes—reducing external engagement while performing regulation, consolidation, repair, and internal processing.

Drago’s counterpunch: “efficiency” can become an excuse for blindness. A sense that was unnecessary yesterday may become decisive after the environment changes. So the optimal system doesn’t keep every channel fully open; it preserves cheap sentinels capable of waking expensive machinery.

Not perceive everything. Remain awakenable by what matters.